WEBVTT

1
00:00:01.880 --> 00:00:05.380
Here is an interesting needle-out-of-work tuck

2
00:00:05.410 --> 00:00:10.540
stitch that's easy to do
and creates a light, open fabric.

3
00:00:10.570 --> 00:00:14.980
This technique is often called tuck lace.

4
00:00:15.010 --> 00:00:18.780
Sometimes tuck can get
a bit heavy and dense.

5
00:00:18.810 --> 00:00:25.260
Keeping needles out of work can help
to create a more lightweight fabric.

6
00:00:25.290 --> 00:00:27.940
All three of these images
are the same swatch.

7
00:00:27.970 --> 00:00:33.980
Right side, wrong side, wrong
side, the choice is yours.

8
00:00:34.000 --> 00:00:39.860
I've cast on over every other
needle and knit two rows.

9
00:00:39.890 --> 00:00:45.320
Now, remember, tuck needs weight,
so not only will I weight my cast on edge,

10
00:00:45.350 --> 00:00:51.740
I'll move claw weights up the side
of my knitting as it grows.

11
00:00:51.770 --> 00:00:54.540
Following my pattern, I'm manually pulling

12
00:00:54.570 --> 00:00:59.580
every-other-needle in work
to hold position.

13
00:00:59.610 --> 00:01:01.820
Set the carriage to hold

14
00:01:01.850 --> 00:01:11.360
and tuck for 4 rows.

15
00:01:16.600 --> 00:01:21.680
Then I'll cancel hold
and knit one closing row.

16
00:01:26.640 --> 00:01:28.180
For the next sequence,

17
00:01:28.210 --> 00:01:31.850
I'll keep those same needles
in out of work position,

18
00:01:31.880 --> 00:01:39.420
but this time I'll pull the opposite
working needles into hold.

19
00:01:39.450 --> 00:01:42.570
Make sure my carriage is set to hold,

20
00:01:42.600 --> 00:01:45.060
those needles are all the way out

21
00:01:45.090 --> 00:01:51.180
into hold position,
knit four rows and knit a closing row.

22
00:01:51.210 --> 00:01:58.700
Now, alternating the tuck stitches
gives the smocking effect that you see.

23
00:01:58.730 --> 00:02:01.380
Notice that I'm not
tucking the edge needles.

24
00:02:01.410 --> 00:02:07.180
This will give me a good
solid edge for seaming.

25
00:02:07.210 --> 00:02:14.380
And you can see the little extra
weight on those edges really helps.

26
00:02:14.410 --> 00:02:17.140
I hope you'll give this
stitch pattern a try.

27
00:02:17.170 --> 00:02:20.980
It's a pretty one, not hard to do,

28
00:02:21.010 --> 00:02:25.440
and will open up your eyes to some
possibilities of tuck lace.

